{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-336198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/assets/b437d93e812512302f48421f23fb048f2cfb9569f7eb3577449a24dfebdfec50.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3=30I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+30I=3.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+30{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-30t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+30\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-30t}+\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-30(0)}+\\frac{1}{10} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{9}{10}{e}^{-30t}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(9{e}^{-30t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=\\frac{9}{10}{e}^{-30t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{10}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "489f44781fa23544ffbff9bffd4bf4fffe1c2707f3939759adc9046a5940b52a"} {"question" "q-336198" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336198" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.1+0.9e^{-30t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/assets/9f527104795b8d264b0d526ccd6c2cd5c2790e4833fa24b169703170715efe12.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $2\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6=24I+2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+12{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+12\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-12t}+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-12(0)}+\\frac{1}{4} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-12t}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(7{e}^{-12t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-12t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{4}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "489f44781fa23544ffbff9bffd4bf4fffe1c2707f3939759adc9046a5940b52a"} {"question" "q-336650" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336650" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.25+1.75e^{-12t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $18\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.05$ seconds given that the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.05$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n9=18I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=3.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-6(0)}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-6t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.05$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.05)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-6(0.05)}+1)≈0.87\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "489f44781fa23544ffbff9bffd4bf4fffe1c2707f3939759adc9046a5940b52a"} {"question" "q-336654" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336654" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.87"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/assets/00d6676b631bd49c0c9b8a82f8dd05de3be6842f18b0c1c6b70f208e1d290d5a.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-12t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=6I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+12{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $5t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $5t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+(12\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +12\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 12\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{30}{169},$ $\\beta =\\frac{72}{169}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{30}{169}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{72}{169}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-12t}-\\frac{30}{169}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{72}{169}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{30}{169},\\,\\beta =\\frac{72}{169}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{30}{169}+\\frac{72}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "489f44781fa23544ffbff9bffd4bf4fffe1c2707f3939759adc9046a5940b52a"} {"question" "q-336741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{38}{169}"] ["math" "\\frac{40}{169}"] ["math" "\\frac{42}{169}"] ["math" "\\frac{44}{169}"] ["math" "\\frac{45}{169}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{42}{169}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/assets/1a2cc9b62f3eeed4f2c9559c7f8cc5646872bc43b8076bea92bae50c3c6b29a9.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=8I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=8I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(8\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +8\\beta =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{6}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}}≈1.34\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ee1693f138c8963fc3e810e69d746cc799d5f8c71d550edbc727cbcc182158c"} {"question" "q-336818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/activity-metadata